WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.700
As we get closer and closer to the end of&nbsp;
the year, we wanted to remind you again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:12.636
that we have 2023 Microcosmos calendars&nbsp;
available at complexlycalendars.com.

00:00:12.636 --> 00:00:18.660
They’re filled with beautiful photos taken by our&nbsp;
Master of Microscopes James Weiss, and each page&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.535
has information about the organism on display,&nbsp;
so you know what you’re looking at each month.

00:00:24.535 --> 00:00:30.000
So, if you’d like to have beautiful microbes on&nbsp;
your wall all throughout 2023, make sure to head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.146
over to complexlycalendars.com&nbsp;
and pick yours up today.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:03.000
Sometimes our journey through the microcosmos&nbsp;
feels like an expedition, a voyage filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.940
deep dives into the masses of organisms&nbsp;
basking under the glow of our microscope.

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:16.380
But at other times, this journey feels&nbsp;
like one big game of hide and seek—one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:22.202
microbes have been winning in part because for&nbsp;
millennia, we didn’t even know we were playing.

00:01:22.202 --> 00:01:24.240
Microbes had managed the ultimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.285
trick of being ubiquitous and yet&nbsp;
completely hidden from our view.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.714
Thanks to the microscope, we’ve managed&nbsp;
to level the playing field a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.714 --> 00:01:37.682
rendering them and their world visible.

00:01:37.682 --> 00:01:43.380
And with patience, James—our master of&nbsp;
microscopes—has become pretty adept at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:47.102
finding microbes in all sorts of unique locations.

00:01:47.552 --> 00:01:53.580
In fact, it feels almost like the tides&nbsp;
have shifted in this game of hide and seek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.089
as if it’s impossible for microbes&nbsp;
to truly hide from us anymore.

00:01:58.089 --> 00:02:03.886
When we peer through the microscope, it seems&nbsp;
like we should always be finding something.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:11.700
So what does it mean when you don’t find anything.
When you gather your samples and excitedly prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:18.000
them for the microscope, only to find a landscape&nbsp;
lacking in the life you expected to find?

00:02:18.796 --> 00:02:23.640
That’s the question that James faced&nbsp;
recently after receiving samples from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.260
a very interesting place: the&nbsp;
bottom of a Norwegian fjord.

00:02:33.975 --> 00:02:40.490
The researchers who sent James the samples are&nbsp;
studying the jellyfish that live in the fjords.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:46.645
But of course, the organisms he was interested&nbsp;
in were on the much smaller side of things.

00:02:47.198 --> 00:02:54.000
Fjords are the result of ancient glaciers that&nbsp;
carved out valleys and filled them with water.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.516
The water in fjords can run very deep,&nbsp;
sometimes on the order of thousands of meters.

00:03:00.516 --> 00:03:04.680
And the steep cliffs that run&nbsp;
along the sides add to the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:10.774
that you are suspended in the middle of&nbsp;
a world that is immense compared to you.

00:03:10.774 --> 00:03:17.160
As small as we are compared to these depths&nbsp;
and heights, there is a tinier world buried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.560
in all this—one that James was excited to&nbsp;
see when he received around 30 tubes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:30.888
sediment samples gathered from depths ranging&nbsp;
from 50 meters to a crushing 1300 meters.

00:03:31.421 --> 00:03:36.752
But when James opened the first tube, he&nbsp;
had to contend with something unexpected:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.778 --> 00:03:41.494
there was no water in the sediment.

00:03:41.494 --> 00:03:44.208
And when he went to dig into the samples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.208 --> 00:03:49.594
they were relatively barren, with&nbsp;
only a few organisms appearing.

00:03:49.594 --> 00:03:52.271
This might not surprise you.

00:03:52.271 --> 00:03:55.541
After all, these are cold, dark waters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.541 --> 00:04:00.840
conditions that should make it hard for&nbsp;
organisms to survive, let alone thrive.

00:04:01.416 --> 00:04:08.517
Yet here we are, talking about fjords, and&nbsp;
there are several minutes left in this video.

00:04:08.517 --> 00:04:12.420
So surely James found something, right?

00:04:13.336 --> 00:04:15.203
Yes, yes he did.

00:04:15.203 --> 00:04:20.116
This is our tour through the depths&nbsp;
of a fjord, told in microbes.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:30.362
Let’s begin with the first 200 meters of the&nbsp;
fjord, the area called the “sunlight” zone.

00:04:30.362 --> 00:04:35.640
As you might guess from that name, this is&nbsp;
an area with light, making it the ideal zone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:42.696
for organisms that rely on sunlight to drive&nbsp;
photosynthesis, like the beautiful diatoms.

00:04:42.696 --> 00:04:45.080
In the Arctic fjords of Svalbard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.513
these surface waters are usually shaped&nbsp;
by meltwater that comes from glaciers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.513 --> 00:04:57.322
as well as runoff from land, making them lower&nbsp;
in salinity compared to the water beneath them.

00:04:57.322 --> 00:05:03.240
As you dive deeper, you can see some of the&nbsp;
remains from organisms that prefer those upper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:10.163
waters—like these frustules that came from&nbsp;
sediment samples taken at 322 meters deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.305
placing them in the area&nbsp;
called the “twilight” zone.

00:05:14.305 --> 00:05:18.780
This region starts at around 200&nbsp;
meters deep and goes down to around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:24.789
1000 meters deep, and while some photons&nbsp;
are able to penetrate these waters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:29.773
they are not enough to guarantee the&nbsp;
survival of photosynthetic organisms.

00:05:29.773 --> 00:05:35.880
But that doesn’t mean other creatures can’t&nbsp;
find a way to survive, like these foraminifera.

00:05:36.678 --> 00:05:43.749
And descending beyond the “twilight” zone takes&nbsp;
you to...yes, you guessed it, the “midnight” zone.

00:05:43.749 --> 00:05:49.587
There is no light here, just dark and&nbsp;
cold waters that, at least in Svalbard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.587 --> 00:05:52.527
accumulate salt released by sea ice.

00:05:53.021 --> 00:05:59.439
Again, we find some of the frustules left behind&nbsp;
by diatoms that lived in the sunlight zone.

00:05:59.439 --> 00:06:05.762
But we also find more unusual&nbsp;
sights. Like this...thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.762 --> 00:06:10.086
which we think is a tube built by a tubeworm.

00:06:10.086 --> 00:06:12.480
Running almost a centimeter in length,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:19.140
the tube was assembled from bits and pieces&nbsp;
of sponge spicules and foraminifera shells.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:23.861
It’s like a collage crafted&nbsp;
out of other fjord microbes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.861 --> 00:06:27.537
telling us more about what&nbsp;
else lived in those waters.

00:06:27.976 --> 00:06:31.943
It’s fascinating, but not surprising, to find the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.943 --> 00:06:37.176
populations of microbes changing&nbsp;
as you dive deeper into the water.

00:06:37.176 --> 00:06:41.640
After all, microbial communities&nbsp;
are shaped by light, temperature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:46.950
salinity, and other factors in&nbsp;
all sorts of bodies of water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:52.209
whether we’re talking about a&nbsp;
fountain, a puddle, a pond, or a fjord.

00:06:52.209 --> 00:06:56.340
We all have preferences for the types&nbsp;
of conditions we want to live in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.975
and microbes are no different.

00:06:58.975 --> 00:07:05.413
With that said, there was one creature that&nbsp;
we seemed to observe at every depth of water.

00:07:05.413 --> 00:07:10.285
In fact, it was often the only&nbsp;
living thing James could find.

00:07:10.285 --> 00:07:12.048
Can you guess what it is?

00:07:17.829 --> 00:07:24.154
It is none other than the nematode! And&nbsp;
perhaps that should not be so surprising.

00:07:24.154 --> 00:07:29.381
They are, after all, one of the most&nbsp;
abundant organisms on the planet.

00:07:29.381 --> 00:07:34.827
And interestingly, the nematodes that&nbsp;
James found from the deeper parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.827 --> 00:07:40.084
the sea had little structures in their&nbsp;
bodies that looked like oil deposits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.084 --> 00:07:45.159
though we’re not exactly sure what they&nbsp;
are or what their function might be.

00:07:45.713 --> 00:07:52.306
Perhaps today we’ve managed a few small&nbsp;
victories in this ongoing game of hide and seek.

00:07:52.306 --> 00:07:59.072
But there are connotations to these games that&nbsp;
extend beyond just simple curiosity and joy.

00:07:59.072 --> 00:08:04.571
The microbes that live in these fjords&nbsp;
are part of a complex ecosystem.

00:08:04.571 --> 00:08:11.328
They cycle elements like carbon, sulfur, and&nbsp;
iron, and they serve as food for other creatures.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.134
So as glaciers retreat due to climate change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.134 --> 00:08:19.479
these communities will have to&nbsp;
contend with a shifting landscape.

00:08:19.479 --> 00:08:22.500
The way water circulates&nbsp;
through the fjord could change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:28.638
bringing different nutrients to the surface&nbsp;
or lifting sediment that blocks more light.

00:08:28.638 --> 00:08:34.917
Or it may bring some other complication for&nbsp;
these microbes that we can’t yet anticipate.

00:08:34.917 --> 00:08:40.440
Scientists are seeking out and studying the&nbsp;
microbial communities in fjords so they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:46.620
better understand what these changes will look&nbsp;
like, to decipher what survival and adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:53.234
looks like at the microbial scale and how&nbsp;
that translates to the world we experience.

00:08:53.234 --> 00:08:55.947
Because microbes will survive.

00:08:55.947 --> 00:09:00.600
They’ve survived plenty of extinctions&nbsp;
already, adapting to the new worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:05.067
that arise after and shaping them for&nbsp;
the evolving forms that join them.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:17.065
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:23.160
And speaking of surviving, the reason&nbsp;
this channel can survive is thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

